INFO  [Claude Code] ## Summary
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] I've successfully resolved all 5 Diffblue Cover issues related to `SocketIOJSONSubscriber` test generation. All issues shared the same root cause: Spring ApplicationContext failures when trying to initialize test contexts.
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ### Issues Fixed
INFO  [Claude Code] 1. `SocketIOJSONSubscriber.disconnect()`
INFO  [Claude Code] 2. `SocketIOJSONSubscriber.internalConnect(URI)`
INFO  [Claude Code] 3. `SocketIOJSONSubscriber.setDefaultTopic(String)`
INFO  [Claude Code] 4. `SocketIOJSONSubscriber.subscribe(String)`
INFO  [Claude Code] 5. `SocketIOJSONSubscriber.unsubscribe(String)`
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ### Root Cause
INFO  [Claude Code] The problems occurred because:
INFO  [Claude Code] - Diffblue Cover was trying to use `TradeFeedHandler` as a Spring configuration class, but it's not annotated as one
INFO  [Claude Code] - `SocketIOJSONSubscriber` implements `InitializingBean` and attempts to connect to a socket server during initialization
INFO  [Claude Code] - During test context loading, no socket server exists, causing initialization failures
INFO  [Claude Code] - Spring's context cache detected the failure and refused to retry
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ### Solution
INFO  [Claude Code] Updated `SocketIOJSONSubscriberDiffblueBase` to provide a proper Spring test configuration that:
INFO  [Claude Code] - Defines valid `@Bean` methods for all required dependencies
INFO  [Claude Code] - Creates a `TradeFeedHandler` instance that overrides `afterPropertiesSet()` to prevent socket connections during test initialization
INFO  [Claude Code] - Mocks the `TradeService` dependency
INFO  [Claude Code] - Uses reflection to inject dependencies
INFO  [Claude Code] - Returns the correct type for autowiring
INFO  [Claude Code] 
